=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a pair of injected `iptables` FORWARD rules on **dswa1**, the Office-A distribution switch. The rules, applied to traffic arriving on the `br0.10` VLAN-10 interface, first ACCEPT ICMP from `10.1.0.0/24` to `10.3.0.0/24`, then DROP all remaining protocols for the same source/destination pair. The practical effect is that every TCP and UDP flow originating from Office-A VLAN-10 clients (including pc1 at 10.1.0.100) and destined for the Office-B VLAN-10 user subnet (10.3.0.0/24) is silently discarded; ICMP pings pass, creating a deceptive "ping works, connections don't" symptom. Packet counters on dswa1 confirmed the rules were hit by exactly the test traffic: 4 ICMP packets accepted, 2 TCP SYN packets dropped.

Crucially, the ticket's stated symptom is **incorrect**: srv1 at 10.5.0.4 (the 10.5.0.0/24 server segment) is fully reachable from pc1 at both ICMP and TCP levels — neither iptables rule covers that destination. The core links, inter-site OSPF adjacencies, and all distribution routing tables are healthy; OSPF is fully converged with full-state neighbors on every distribution-to-core link.

The root cause is the insertion of two iptables FORWARD rules on dswa1 that block non-ICMP traffic between Office-A and Office-B VLAN 10. The fix is to delete both rules on dswa1: `iptables -D FORWARD -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -i br0.10 -p icmp -j ACCEPT` followed by `iptables -D FORWARD -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -i br0.10 -j DROP`.
=== END ===